  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1233 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nStrong thunderstorms are currently propagating southward along a\ncold front across West Tennessee, with outflow-driven convection\nextending into portions of southeast Arkansas and northwest\nMississippi. The primary hazards associated with this activity\ninclude gusty winds, heavy rainfall, and intense lightning.\nDamaging wind gusts remain a possibility through the next several\nhours, particularly over West Tennessee. The severe weather\nthreat is expected to diminish overnight as the primary jet\nstreak exits the region and instability wanes.\n\nOn Wednesday, the front is forecast to stall before eventually\npushing south by morning, ushering in a cooler and drier airmass\nfor much of the area. Despite the frontal passage, heat indices\nof 105F or higher are anticipated in southern counties, where a\nHeat Advisory will be in effect from 10 AM to 8 PM. By Thursday,\nnoticeably drier air will keep heat indices near ambient\ntemperatures, with afternoon highs ranging 5-10 degrees below\nseasonal norms.\n\nTropical Storm Bertha is forecast to make landfall in southeast\nTexas Thursday night. In its wake, a subtropical ridge will\nretrograde westward while broad troughing develops over the Ohio\nValley, placing the Mid-South in a northwest flow regime through\nthe weekend. A marine frontal passage on Saturday will likely\nbring a return of elevated heat and humidity.\n\nCurrent model guidance suggests that shortwaves embedded within\nthe northwest flow may trigger a Mesoscale Convective System\n(MCS) late Friday night. However, confidence regarding severe\npotential remains low due to significant spread in LREF and\ndeterministic guidance. By early next week, long-range guidance\nindicates the subtropical ridge will build eastward, leading to\nincreasing heat and humidity. NBM 50th percentile values suggest\napparent temperatures will climb well above the 100-degree mark\nareawide during this period.",
